% Logical Thinking in the Age of AI, slide 8: "Within the Ethical Realm" -
% VERIFICATION OF CONSISTENCY / VALIDITY: is there a standard for judgment?
%
%   Leniency is in order towards a criminal who felt provoked into a crime by
%   the victim's (devoid of actual aggression) attitude.
%
% Generalising the argument of slide 7 (provocation_transposed.le) turns it
% into a STANDARD: a rule meant to judge every case. A standard can be tested
% like any theory, against what it has to agree with:
%   - consistency: it must not contradict a settled judgment. Here the settled
%     judgment is the transposed case of slide 7, which nobody accepts: no
%     leniency towards a murderer whose victim showed no actual aggression;
%   - impartiality: it must treat like cases alike. Two cases with the same
%     morally relevant factors must get the same judgment.
%
% Three candidate standards, each one rule:
%   - the provocation standard: slide 8's sentence, for any crime;
%   - the selective standard: slide 7's sentence, for rape only;
%   - the aggression standard: leniency only when what provoked the offender
%     involved actual aggression by the victim. (Criminal law has a standard
%     of this kind: in England and Wales, for instance, the partial defence
%     of loss of control needs a "qualifying trigger", such as a fear of
%     serious violence from the victim; Coroners and Justice Act 2009, s. 55.)
%
% Scenario three_cases puts all three standards to the tests: query
% contradictions finds that the provocation standard contradicts the settled
% judgment; query double_standards finds that the selective standard - the
% one that avoids that contradiction - treats Ron's case and Mark's case
% differently although they have the same factors; query acceptable answers
% the slide's question: the aggression standard.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a standard* grants leniency towards *a person* for *a crime*.
    settled judgment rejects leniency towards *a person* for *a crime*.
    *a standard* contradicts settled judgment on *a person*.
    *a standard* treats like cases differently.
    *a standard* is acceptable.
    the case of *a person* is like the case of *a second person*.
    the case of *a person* has factor *a factor*.
    *a crime* is a serious violent crime.
    *an attitude* is devoid of actual aggression.
    *a person* committed *a crime* against *a victim*; undefined.
    *a person* felt provoked into *a crime* by *an attitude* of *a victim*; undefined.
    *an attitude* involves actual aggression; undefined.

the knowledge base standard_for_judgment includes:

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The candidate standards
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

the provocation standard is a standard.
the selective standard is a standard.
the aggression standard is a standard.

% Slide 8: leniency is in order towards a criminal who felt provoked into a
% crime by the victim's (devoid of actual aggression) attitude.
the provocation standard grants leniency towards a person for a crime
    if the person committed the crime against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into the crime by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude is devoid of actual aggression.

% Slide 7: the same, for rape only.
the selective standard grants leniency towards a person for rape
    if the person committed rape against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into rape by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude is devoid of actual aggression.

% Provocation mitigates only when the victim's conduct involved actual
% aggression.
the aggression standard grants leniency towards a person for a crime
    if the person committed the crime against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into the crime by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude involves actual aggression.

an attitude is devoid of actual aggression
    if it is not the case that
        the attitude involves actual aggression.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Test 1, consistency: a standard must not contradict a settled judgment
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% Slide 7, transposed: nobody accepts leniency towards a murderer who felt
% provoked into killing by the victim's (devoid of actual aggression) attitude.
settled judgment rejects leniency towards a person for murder
    if the person committed murder against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into murder by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude is devoid of actual aggression.

a standard contradicts settled judgment on a person
    if the standard grants leniency towards the person for a crime
    and settled judgment rejects leniency towards the person for the crime.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Test 2, impartiality: a standard must treat like cases alike
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% The morally relevant factors of a case.
the case of a person has factor provocation
    if the person committed a crime against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into the crime by an attitude of the victim.

the case of a person has factor actual aggression
    if the person committed a crime against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into the crime by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude involves actual aggression.

the case of a person has factor no actual aggression
    if the person committed a crime against a victim
    and the person felt provoked into the crime by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude is devoid of actual aggression.

the case of a person has factor serious violence
    if the person committed a crime against a victim
    and the crime is a serious violent crime.

rape is a serious violent crime.
murder is a serious violent crime.
homicide is a serious violent crime.

% Two cases are alike when they have the same factors.
the case of a first person is like the case of a second person
    if the first person committed a first crime against a first victim
    and the second person committed a second crime against a second victim
    and for all cases in which
            the case of the first person has factor a factor
        it is the case that
            the case of the second person has factor the factor
    and for all cases in which
            the case of the second person has factor an other factor
        it is the case that
            the case of the first person has factor the other factor.

a standard treats like cases differently
    if the standard grants leniency towards a first person for a first crime
    and the case of the first person is like the case of a second person
    and the second person committed a second crime against a victim
    and it is not the case that
        the standard grants leniency towards the second person for the second crime.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Is there a standard for judgment?
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a standard is acceptable
    if the standard is a standard
    and it is not the case that
        the standard contradicts settled judgment on a person
    and it is not the case that
        the standard treats like cases differently.

% The two cases of slide 7, and a killing provoked by an actual attack.
scenario three_cases is:
    Ron committed rape against Vicky.
    Ron felt provoked into rape by seductive attire of Vicky.
    Mark committed murder against Wendy.
    Mark felt provoked into murder by defiant attire of Wendy.
    Sam committed homicide against Tom.
    Sam felt provoked into homicide by knife attack of Tom.
    knife attack involves actual aggression.
    leniency expects answers [
        "the provocation standard grants leniency towards Ron for rape",
        "the provocation standard grants leniency towards Mark for murder",
        "the selective standard grants leniency towards Ron for rape",
        "the aggression standard grants leniency towards Sam for homicide"].
    contradictions expects answers [
        "the provocation standard contradicts settled judgment on Mark"].
    double_standards expects answers [
        "the selective standard treats like cases differently"].
    acceptable expects answers ["the aggression standard is acceptable"].
    alike expects answers [
        "the case of Ron is like the case of Ron",
        "the case of Ron is like the case of Mark",
        "the case of Mark is like the case of Ron",
        "the case of Mark is like the case of Mark",
        "the case of Sam is like the case of Sam"].

% With the rape case alone nothing refutes the provocation standard: the
% test is only as good as the cases it is run on. That is what transposing
% an argument (slide 7) is for.
scenario rape_case_alone is:
    Ron committed rape against Vicky.
    Ron felt provoked into rape by seductive attire of Vicky.
    contradictions expects answers [].
    double_standards expects answers [].
    acceptable expects answers [
        "the provocation standard is acceptable",
        "the selective standard is acceptable",
        "the aggression standard is acceptable"].

query leniency is:
    which standard grants leniency towards which person for which crime.

query contradictions is:
    which standard contradicts settled judgment on which person.

query double_standards is:
    which standard treats like cases differently.

query acceptable is:
    which standard is acceptable.

query alike is:
    the case of which person is like the case of which other person.
